API Request Structure
______________<__________________________________<_____________________
*​Every API request is made up of several key parts working together.
​
The Basic Flow
​1. REQUEST (Client ➔ Server): 
Consists of Endpoint + Headers + Params + Body

​2. RESPONSE (Server ➔ Client): 
Consists of Status Code + Headers + Body

​1. ENDPOINT (URL)
​Definition: The URL that identifies the resource. It is composed of the Base URL + path.
​Example: [https://api.example.com/users/101](https://api.example.com/users/101)
​Base URL: [https://api.example.com](https://api.example.com)
​Path: /users/101

​2. HEADERS
​Definition: Key-value pairs that send extra information.
​Common Headers: Authorization, Accept, Content-Type.
​Example:
​Authorization: Bearer token123
​Accept: application/json
​Content-Type: application/json

​3. PATH PARAMETERS
​Definition: Values included in the URL path to identify a resource. Usually represents a unique ID.
​Example: [https://api.example.com/users/101](https://api.example.com/users/101)
​Path Parameter: 101

​4. QUERY PARAMETERS
​Definition: Key-value pairs added at the end of the URL. Used for filtering, sorting, and pagination.
​Example: [https://api.example.com/users?page=1&limit=10](https://api.example.com/users?page=1&limit=10)
​Query Parameters: ?page=1&limit=10

5. REQUEST BODY
Definition: Data sent to the server. 
Used in POST, PUT, and PATCH requests. 
Usually in JSON or XML format.
Example (JSON):
{
  "name": "John",
  "email": "john@gmail.com",
  "role": "QA Engineer"
}

6. CONTENT-TYPE
Definition: Tells the server the format of the request body.
​Common Types: application/json, application/xml, multipart/form-data.
​Example: Content-Type: application/json

7. What is a Mock API?
Answer:
• A Mock API is a simulated API that mimics the behavior of a real API.
• It is used during development and testing before the real backend is ready.

8. What is Versioning in API?
Answer:
• API versioning is the process of updating APIs without breaking existing functionality.
• Version information can be included in the URL or request headers.
• Example: /api/v1/users

9. What is Latency in API?
Answer:
• Latency is the time taken between sending a request and receiving a response.
• It is usually measured in milliseconds (ms).
• Lower latency means faster API performance and a better user experience.

10. What is API Gateway?
Answer:
• An API Gateway is a server that acts as the front door for APIs.
• It handles authentication, routing, rate limiting, logging, etc.
• Examples: AWS API Gateway, Kong, Apigee.

